#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# whittaker_w

#% text
# `whittaker_w(k, m, z)` gives the Whittaker function `W_(k, m)(z) = exp(-z/2)*z^(m + 1/2)*hypergeometric_u(m - k + 1/2, 1 + 2*m, z)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The definition is DLMF 13.14.3; `W` is the solution of Whittaker's equation that decays like `exp(-z/2)*z^k` for large `z`.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
whittaker_w(0.3, 0.5, 1.0)
#% output
#= 0.6978515383358584

#% cell
whittaker_w(0.5, 0.25, 2.0)
#% output
#= 0.534013946067451

#% cell
whittaker_w(1.2, 1.5, 3.0)
#% output
#= 1.5082906339924933

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The definition through `hypergeometric_u`:

#% cell
whittaker_w(0.3, 0.7, 1.2) - exp(-0.6)*1.2^1.2*hypergeometric_u(0.9, 2.4, 1.2)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% text
# `W_(k, m)` is even in `m`:

#% cell
whittaker_w(0.3, 0.7, 1.2) - whittaker_w(0.3, -0.7, 1.2)
#% output
#= 0.0


#% text
# Values where the hypergeometric U is a polynomial:

#% cell
[whittaker_w(1, 0.5, 1.0), whittaker_w(2, 1.5, 3.0)]
#% output
#= [0.6065306597126334, 2.0081714413358682]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `whittaker_m`, `hypergeometric_u`, `hypergeometric_1f1`
